WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.760
OpenAI's GPT 6.1 Terra delivers faster computer use agents at half the prior token price. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 216. Open ESM weights go public, agent memory papers cut token spend, then things to try. OpenAI is rolling GPT 6.1 Terra out to chat GPT work, codex, and the API under the name GPT 6.1- Terra. The model understands screen content, performs actions across applications,

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.340
and writes naturally.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.860
It matches GPT-6 Astra on Agents last exam

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.700
at one-tenth the price per token.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.820
Tasks finish over three times faster on average

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.620
than both Astra and GPT-6.1 sole.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.240
In chat GPT work and Codex,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.980
the usage allowance stretches further

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.140
than with GPT-5.6 Terra.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.400
Developers can access it immediately

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.060
through the API or chat GPT interfaces

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.300
for agent-style work.

00:00:55.323 --> 00:00:57.083
Energy steering meets token generation

00:00:57.083 --> 00:01:01.183
in Open ESM, one architecture described in an archive paper.

00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:05.283
The model first optimizes a latent state over an energy landscape

00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:08.003
then uses that refined state for generation.

00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:11.203
Pre-training ran on three large-scale corpora.

00:01:11.823 --> 00:01:14.463
Evaluation covered 26 downstream tasks,

00:01:14.763 --> 00:01:17.623
where the models outperform GPTs on most of them

00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:19.543
and show promising scaling behavior.

00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:21.883
The team fully releases model weights

00:01:21.883 --> 00:01:24.623
and training infrastructure on GitHub and Hugging Face.

00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:30.849
Next token training gets a meaning reward in conceptizer. A fixed vocabulary of about

00:01:30.849 --> 00:01:37.489
9,000 contextual synonym concepts sits alongside ordinary tokens. The model receives a reward

00:01:37.489 --> 00:01:43.389
for meaning rather than only the exact next token. Tests on 1 billion to 8 billion parameter

00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:48.509
models across three families post the lowest held out perplexity at concept target positions.

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:54.649
The same runs record the highest probability on contextual synonyms. Downstream gains appear

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:59.169
on knowledge and sentiment tasks, while common sense performance stays comparable.

00:02:00.257 --> 00:02:05.557
Nepali vocabulary work uses a small rejects edit to the pre-tokenizer that keeps every

00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:09.397
token ID of text without combining marks or divanigari characters.

00:02:10.037 --> 00:02:16.577
The repair, with 32,000 new merges, reduces Nepali token count to 1.01 times English.

00:02:17.297 --> 00:02:23.657
Nepali tokens drop by about 54% versus standard vocabulary extension on Lama 3 and QN3.

00:02:23.657 --> 00:02:31.537
Continued pre-training on Lama 3.21B and QN3 0.6B still leaves the original tokenizer

00:02:31.537 --> 00:02:35.337
winning on held out Napoli bits per bite under every tested recipe.

00:02:36.384 --> 00:02:39.924
Indic language tokenization is the target of Adapt Talk Indic.

00:02:40.464 --> 00:02:45.544
Unicode normalize pre-processing and explicit Indic conjunct handling sit alongside script

00:02:45.544 --> 00:02:46.284
aware routing.

00:02:47.024 --> 00:02:49.844
Learned bite-level boundary prediction completes the stack.

00:02:49.844 --> 00:02:58.964
mean fertility, tokens per word more or less hits 1.43 on Bengali, and 1.388 on Hindi, well

00:02:58.964 --> 00:03:06.484
below sentence piece bite pair encoding, or BPE. Downstream scores average 57.69 on evaluated

00:03:06.484 --> 00:03:13.942
index settings, versus 50.82 for the sentence piece baseline. Recursive semantic divergence measures

00:03:13.942 --> 00:03:19.402
expected cumulative semantic disagreement between independent rollouts of the same agent from the same

00:03:19.402 --> 00:03:25.102
state using natural language inference. As a reward signal, it cuts task progress variation

00:03:25.102 --> 00:03:35.262
from 132% to 91% on tau2-bench. On tools and box, that variation falls from 90% to 44%.

00:03:35.262 --> 00:03:40.422
The metric needs no ground truth labels. It estimates future divergence from the current state.

00:03:40.882 --> 00:03:47.250
Performance improves across benchmarks and scales. Slicemem uses a compact classifier for

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:52.730
salience guided admission. A semantic dependency extractor converts turns into concise memory

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:58.910
entries without generative, large language model, or LLM calls. On Memora Weekly and Long

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:06.650
Mem EVAL-S with QN 330B backbones, it matches the generative baseline MemOS. Total LLM token

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:15.070
consumption falls by 92.4% on 1 and 99.5% on the other. Cross-entry reconciliation is deferred

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:20.888
to an offline stage. FlexA Mem writes each unit of history once into an immutable store.

00:04:21.348 --> 00:04:26.568
Every call gets a fresh, compact timeline plus detail, rendered by a fixed rule that

00:04:26.568 --> 00:04:33.188
fits remaining budget without an LLM call. On app world, at a 4,000 token context, it outperforms

00:04:33.188 --> 00:04:41.988
the second best method by 11.5%. On 147 unused tasks, it beats A Mem with summary by 12.2%.

00:04:41.988 --> 00:04:45.968
The timeline carries completion while recovery mainly saves steps.

00:04:46.988 --> 00:04:51.508
Diffusion language models are the setting for trace query, which predicts a small set

00:04:51.508 --> 00:04:55.568
of near-future trajectory relevant positions from full denoising trajectories.

00:04:56.068 --> 00:04:57.748
It queries only those candidates.

00:04:58.288 --> 00:05:03.308
That yields the strongest quality computation trade-off among cash-based approaches for diffusion

00:05:03.308 --> 00:05:04.048
LM's.

00:05:04.548 --> 00:05:08.148
The method generalizes across tasks, model variants, and families.

00:05:08.948 --> 00:05:10.148
Code is released on GitHub.

00:05:10.148 --> 00:05:15.763
club. Historical document transcription gets an entity-aware evaluation of optical character

00:05:15.763 --> 00:05:21.923
recognition or OCR, judged at the entity level rather than raw character match. The benchmark

00:05:21.923 --> 00:05:29.743
covers 175 documents from the Barudi archive. Annotators marked 5209 entity mentions by hand.

00:05:30.443 --> 00:05:36.963
QN 3.6 achieves the highest entity coverage across tolerances. Several lower exact match systems

00:05:36.963 --> 00:05:40.263
recovers substantially when small character errors are tolerated.

00:05:40.623 --> 00:05:43.663
The benchmark and annotations will be released upon acceptance.

00:05:44.861 --> 00:05:49.301
Automatic speech recognition fine-tuning is the problem whispering wisely attacks with

00:05:49.301 --> 00:05:53.381
embedding-based case-center subset selection on whispers pre-trained encoder.

00:05:53.921 --> 00:05:59.201
On Hebrew and Danish, the approach often beats random sampling, especially at smaller subset

00:05:59.201 --> 00:05:59.721
sizes.

00:06:00.281 --> 00:06:05.161
Those case-center subsets capture broader lexical diversity, which helps when transcribed

00:06:05.161 --> 00:06:06.161
audio is limited.

00:06:06.161 --> 00:06:12.347
Speaking-style captioning gets its first standardized instruction following benchmark in style-instruct

00:06:12.347 --> 00:06:12.827
caps.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:18.287
The framework scores metadata groundedness, hallucination, instruction following, and

00:06:18.287 --> 00:06:19.707
speaker-style consistency.

00:06:20.347 --> 00:06:25.887
Across nine state-of-the-art audio-language models, instruction-diverse training substantially improves

00:06:25.887 --> 00:06:27.627
groundedness and controllability.

00:06:28.556 --> 00:06:34.916
Chest-computed tomography reports generated by LLMs are the target of EV ground CT, which extracts

00:06:34.916 --> 00:06:39.376
positive finding sentences and predicts a three-dimensional evidence mask for each one.

00:06:39.956 --> 00:06:47.576
Sentence-grounded verification raises evidence-supported precision from 0.263 to 0.365 on a held out

00:06:47.576 --> 00:06:55.136
100-case subset. The unsupported positive finding rate falls from 0.737 to 0.635.

00:06:56.226 --> 00:07:01.526
Elsewhere, industry experts tell business standard that AI agents could transform banking operations

00:07:01.526 --> 00:07:05.806
while remaining risks sit around security, compliance, and oversight.

00:07:06.466 --> 00:07:12.726
Individuals and teams beginning to experiment with AI agents get practical first steps in a Washington Post analysis.

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:20.546
Oil and gas operations could see agentic AI connect workflows and accelerate decisions, according to oil and gas journal.

00:07:21.126 --> 00:07:30.046
Anthropic filed an S1 registration statement with the Securities and Exchange Commission or SEC for its proposed initial public offering or IPO.

00:07:30.046 --> 00:07:36.686
CodeRabbit uses GPT 6.1 soil to investigate security risks across complex code bases

00:07:36.686 --> 00:07:40.406
and review proposed fixes according to the OpenAI blog.

00:07:41.046 --> 00:07:47.286
GrapTile uses GPT 6 Astra to test user workflows, strengthen security, and reduce rework

00:07:47.286 --> 00:07:48.346
inside CodeX.

00:07:48.966 --> 00:07:56.006
OpenAI signals data covers 165 work activities across nearly 100 countries and shows how chat

00:07:56.006 --> 00:07:58.446
GPT usage for work evolves over time.

00:07:58.446 --> 00:08:04.662
Test GPT 6.1 Terra through the OpenAI API or chat GPT work on

00:08:04.662 --> 00:08:08.662
agentic computer use tasks, where speed and token costs dominate.

00:08:09.342 --> 00:08:13.222
Pull the released OpenESM weights on hugging face and try energy steered

00:08:13.222 --> 00:08:17.682
pre-training on your own corpora. When you fine-tune Whisper on Hebrew or

00:08:17.682 --> 00:08:22.582
Danish with limited transcribed audio, use that K center subset selection instead

00:08:22.582 --> 00:08:27.202
of a random slice. If you work in Bengali or Hindi and your tokenizer is

00:08:27.202 --> 00:08:32.602
to chatty, run Adapt Talk Indic, and compare fertility against sentence-piece BPE.

00:08:33.562 --> 00:08:38.902
Continued GPT 6.1 family releases are expected to push further on the cost intelligence

00:08:38.902 --> 00:08:40.282
curve for agent workloads.

00:08:41.002 --> 00:08:46.082
Additional entity-aware OCR benchmarks for historical documents are anticipated once the

00:08:46.082 --> 00:08:48.142
Barudi Archive annotations are public.

00:08:49.175 --> 00:08:54.615
Soft prompt compression paid a quadratic transformer tax on every extra token, so it is time

00:08:54.615 --> 00:08:56.515
to pop the hood on the Boa compressor.

00:08:56.515 --> 00:09:02.355
Boa replaces that transformer backbone with a Mamba state space model that encodes arbitrary

00:09:02.355 --> 00:09:05.115
length context in a single linear time pass.

00:09:05.315 --> 00:09:10.075
That pass produces a representation which conditions a frozen target LLM.

00:09:10.495 --> 00:09:15.055
The state space model is trained by distillation, so its compressed representation produces

00:09:15.055 --> 00:09:19.575
the same next token logits as the target LLM ingesting the full context.

00:09:20.175 --> 00:09:25.475
Because the compressor runs online on every query instead of once offline, it removes the

00:09:25.475 --> 00:09:30.195
latency overhead that previously confined soft prompt methods to repeated query settings.

00:09:30.675 --> 00:09:35.615
At identical compression ratios, the approach matches or exceeds prior offline transformer

00:09:35.615 --> 00:09:36.595
based baselines.

00:09:37.255 --> 00:09:40.115
Compression time drops by roughly an order of magnitude.

00:09:40.655 --> 00:09:45.935
It continues to scale to 16 times compression and longer contexts without the quadratic cost

00:09:45.935 --> 00:09:50.775
that grows with transformer based compressors, and without adding per query overhead.

00:09:50.775 --> 00:09:56.395
The practical limit appears when the target LLM's own context window becomes the bottleneck

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:57.935
rather than the compressor itself.

00:09:58.595 --> 00:10:02.495
Teams facing repeated long-context queries should test BoA first.

00:10:03.095 --> 00:10:07.675
Teams that compress once and reuse across many queries can still prefer the older offline

00:10:07.675 --> 00:10:08.075
methods.

